WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.867
Exponential change repeats multiplication by a fixed factor

00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:07.333
Label the starting amount three as term one

00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:11.667
Doubling gives six, twelve, and twenty four as later terms

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:16.433
The amounts added grow, but neighboring terms always have ratio two.

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:21.447
Let x now count elapsed steps, beginning at zero

00:00:21.447 --> 00:00:25.573
The continuous graph extends our doubling rule to real inputs

00:00:25.573 --> 00:00:30.112
At zero the factor is one, preserving the initial amount three

00:00:30.112 --> 00:00:33.000
Every additional input unit doubles the output.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.627
A linear comparison instead adds a fixed amount at each step

00:00:38.627 --> 00:00:48.460
Both rules begin at three and agree again at input one, yet then separate because equal starting values do not determine the type of change

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.033
Compare differences and ratios.

00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:54.911
A positive factor below one gives exponential decay

00:00:54.911 --> 00:01:02.514
Each step retains half of the previous amount, approaching zero while staying positive for finite real inputs

00:01:02.514 --> 00:01:07.433
The base must be positive for this standard real exponential function.
